re it. And the students who walked through your mentors' doors had already been shaped by years of that same wisdom from the teachers before them. Of course the outcomes looked consistent. The system was consistent—in its failure to challenge. Now, to your original dilemma: if this really is such settled, accepted wisdom among teachers, why are you worried about how to present it to parents? Presumably, if it's just honest professionalism, you could say it out loud: "I've predicted your child a lower grade because, in my experience and in the data, children from your income bracket don't achieve as highly." You can't say that, and you know you can't, because it's professionally indefensible and morally abhorrent. The fact that you asked how to present these grades suggests that some part of you recognises this isn't something you can defend openly. That instinct is worth listening to. You can break from your mentors. You can be the generation that stops passing this down. Not by pretending poverty doesn't matter, but by refusing to use it as a grade modifier. By being the teacher who says, "I know what the patterns show, but I will judge this child on their own work, and I will move heaven and earth to plug the gaps poverty creates rather than lower the bar." That's not naivety. It's moral clarity. And plenty of teachers in the toughest schools are already doing it, quietly, against the grain of the old guard. The "accepted wisdom" was wrong when your mentors taught it to you, and it's wrong now. Not because I say so, but because writing off a child based on their background is wrong, full stop, no matter how many generations of exhausted teachers have convinced themselves otherwise.
