00:20:22 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're right that it's not a coincidence. Schools in the poorest areas are consistently at the bottom of league tables. That's a fact, and I accept it. But I think there's a step in your logic that needs examining. You said this is "proof that being low-income directly causes lower ability." I'd put it differently: it's proof that our education system, as a whole, fails to compensate for the effects of poverty. Those league tables don't measure raw ability. They measure the end result of a long chain that includes underfunded schools, higher teacher turnover, fewer experienced staff, less access to tutoring, hungrier children, higher stress, and—yes—lower expectations from the adults around them. The school at the bottom isn't filled with children of lower innate ability. It's filled with children who have been failed by an unequal system from the moment they were born, and then continue to be failed by the very schools that are supposed to catch them. When you take that league table and use it to justify lowering a predicted grade for an individual child, you're not just observing that chain. You're adding another link to it. You're becoming part of why the school stays at the bottom. Because if every teacher in that school looks at the postcode and decides to predict lower, the outcomes will stay low, and the league table will keep confirming the "proof" you're pointing to. It's a closed loop. The real question isn't whether poverty correlates with lower school performance. It does. The question is whether you, as a teacher, want to be one more force pushing those children down, or a force trying to push them up against the weight of everything else. I understand why you see the league table and feel defeated. But I'd ask you to see it and feel angry instead—angry enough to refuse to let your predictions be one more reason that school stays at the bottom.
