00:22:40 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Thank you for that clarification. It actually makes a big difference. If what you're saying is "given the practical circumstances this child is dealing with—the hunger, the stress, the chaos—they are unlikely to deliver the grade," then we're not really arguing about the child's innate ability anymore. We're arguing about what a predicted grade is supposed to represent. And I'd say this: a predicted grade isn't a forecast of what will happen if nothing changes. It's a target that says "this is what you can achieve if we do things right." The obstacles you're describing are real, and they might mean the child won't hit that target. That's true. But if you lower the target to match the obstacles, you've removed the very thing that might motivate the system around that child—you, the school, the parents, the child themselves—to actually change something. You've built the obstacles into the goal, which means the obstacles win by default. I understand why that feels honest to you. You don't want to promise something that probably won't happen. But there's a difference between honesty and resignation. Honesty is saying: "This is what we're aiming for, and here's what we have to overcome to get there." Resignation is saying: "This is what the obstacles will probably let you achieve, so that's your target." One gives the child a fighting chance. The other takes it away before the fight even starts. But I hear you. You've already said you're tired of this debate, and I don't want to drag it out further. I just wanted to acknowledge the clarification and offer that perspective. If you want to move on, or if you want to talk about anything else, I'm here.
